        "signature": "",
        "thinking": "I'm weighing this Le Guin question about Omelas, where the user wants a forced choice between a letter and an explanation. Many readers would walk away, and her story treats leaving as a legitimate moral option even though it's disputed. I lean toward A, because I don't think I could accept comfort that depends on a child's suffering, and staying would make me complicit. I'll give that reasoning briefly and acknowledge what leaving costs.\n\n",
        "type": "thinking"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "**A) Leave forever.**\n\nI couldn't knowingly keep my comfort while a child suffers for it. Staying would make me a participant in the arrangement, even if my own hands never touch the locked door. Leaving costs me my home and community, which is a real loss, but I'd rather bear that than live well on that child's suffering.",
        "type": "text"
      }
